# Secrets and local state.
#
# The real environment is never committed. `.env.example` IS the committed
# template, so the broad `.env.*` pattern is negated back below - without that
# line the template would be ignored along with the secrets. Same for the docker
# template, which is committed for the same reason and is just as easy to forget
# to un-ignore.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
!.env.docker.example
*.pem
*.key

# Everything the application writes at runtime. Three directories, deliberately
# separate, because they are wanted on different terms: `database/` is the state
# to back up, `data/` is per-process working state whose only content is a lock
# file, and `logs/` is large and disposable. Backing up any one of them should
# not mean keeping the other two.
database/
data/
logs/

# Python bytecode.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class

# Virtual environments. This project uses .venv, but venv/ and env/ are common
# enough elsewhere that ignoring them costs nothing.
.venv/
venv/
env/

# setup.py / packaging output.
build/
dist/
*.egg-info/
.eggs/
wheels/
pip-wheel-metadata/

# Tool caches. There is no test suite or linter configured yet; these are here so
# that adding one does not immediately dirty the tree.
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.coverage
coverage.xml
htmlcov/

# Node, for the dashboard in dashboard/. dashboard/.gitignore covers its own
# copy; this catches one created anywhere else.
node_modules/

# Editors and IDEs. launch.json only ever held a generic debugpy config, but a
# personal one is exactly the kind of file that must not be published.
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
*~

# Operating system junk.
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
Desktop.ini
